#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9688df89-056e-59d7-869e-9841715b2aea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7a0aba38-1167-5365-bbd2-cd2ba7e57b15" :question/math-academy-id "e-612" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf F(t)$ given that $\\mathbf F'(t)= \\langle 6t^5+2t, \\sec^2 t\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle 6t^5+2t,\\, \\sec^2 t\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨6{t}^{5}+2t,\\,{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int (6{t}^{5}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int {sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2}+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{6}+{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53fca396-4ade-5180-9c9b-a73f24f5b5b8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7a39ddf2-3ba2-534f-8dca-93497cae823f" :question/math-academy-id "e-613" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\left\\langle\\cos(2t+1), \\, 4\\right\\rangle \\,\\textrm d t\\,.$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1),\\,4⟩\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x(t) =\\cos(2t+1),$ we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u= 2t +1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d t = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)+{c}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1)+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t) =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int 4\\text{d}t=4t+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+1),\\,4⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1)+{c}_{1},\\,4t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1),\\,4t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+1),\\,4t⟩+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3081ffe7-c448-5e0d-8dd1-808d23199a7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6da4ce70-1718-53bb-8831-a93fdc3e4677" :question/math-academy-id "e-614" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2} \\left\\langle 2e^{t},\\, 6t^2\\right\\rangle\\, \\textrm d t.$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}⟨2{e}^{t},\\,6{t}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩. \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{t}\\,\\,{|}_{0}^{2},\\,2{t}^{3}\\,\\,{|}_{0}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨[2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{0}],\\,[2\\cdot {2}^{3}-0]⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{2}-2,\\,16⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dd18a5b-d7a1-5590-ae70-f47ff5b464b1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function That Satisfies a Given Initial Condition"}] :question/id #uuid "76a0fefe-71fd-5500-a4d8-7d5f8a09613e" :question/math-academy-id "e-615" :question/problem "Calculate $\\mathbf F(t)$ given that $\\mathbf F'(t)= \\left\\langle \\sec t \\tan t, \\,  \\sin(2t)\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf F(0)=\\langle 0,\\, 0\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle \\sec t \\tan t,\\,  \\sin(2t)\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $\\mathbf F(0)=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0 \\right\\rangle$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0)=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨0,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=\\dfrac 12.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\left\\langle  \\sec t - 1  , \\,  -\\dfrac 1 2\\cos(2t)+ \\dfrac12\\right\\rangle.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53fca396-4ade-5180-9c9b-a73f24f5b5b8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101cbc3d-8ada-5787-9d21-feee751379e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t},\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332923} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce5478b-9a72-55ff-b65d-51d747a90918" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t-1},\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332924} {:answer/id #uuid "eb066872-e293-5a52-8a19-b26ef182a5a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t},\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332925} {:answer/id #uuid "c3220efa-582f-58e2-a10e-77732718dde1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t-1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332926} {:answer/id #uuid "97612383-f607-5ead-84a2-f7aff88224f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{5t},\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "101cbc3d-8ada-5787-9d21-feee751379e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{e}^{t},\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332923} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a90f7e6-ebbb-53f5-b993-94997e7d0347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f7f0bed-7a5e-5c15-8327-bc7f4c63ab8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123437" :question/problem "Find $\\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})⟩\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})⟩\\text{d}t=⟨\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x(t)={e}^{t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\text{d}t={e}^{t}+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u=2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5(\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)+{c}_{2} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨{e}^{t}+{c}_{1},\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})⟩+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dd18a5b-d7a1-5590-ae70-f47ff5b464b1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function That Satisfies a Given Initial Condition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4445b6b7-0280-50d9-ab9d-cc1c2e794140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{t}^{4/3}+2,\\,5{e}^{5t}-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332965} {:answer/id #uuid "7b77aede-9c55-5ec3-9e49-8d826f858596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{t}^{4/3}-2,\\,{e}^{5t}-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332966} {:answer/id #uuid "cac5345a-435b-5d99-86cd-ad99e17a19b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{t}^{4/3}+2,\\,{e}^{5t}-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332967} {:answer/id #uuid "34364ab1-eb6b-513e-b87a-98bf44363d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{t}^{1/3}+1,\\,{e}^{t}-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332968} {:answer/id #uuid "f05d4e77-3f85-5d2a-b440-3d79f4567766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{t}^{4/3}+2,\\,{e}^{2t}-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cac5345a-435b-5d99-86cd-ad99e17a19b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4{t}^{4/3}+2,\\,{e}^{5t}-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332967} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d1f277b-a634-584c-8716-a16b3efc218b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01a82786-6006-50b3-9639-081950d67e5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-123474" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨\\frac{16\\sqrt[3]{t}}{3},\\,5{e}^{5t}⟩$ and $F(0)=⟨2,\\,-1⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\frac{16\\sqrt[3]{t}}{3},\\,5{e}^{5t}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\frac{16\\sqrt[3]{t}}{3},\\,5{e}^{5t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\frac{16\\sqrt[3]{t}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 5{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{4/3}+{c}_{1},\\,{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(0)=⟨2,\\,-1⟩$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0)=⟨4\\cdot {0}^{4/3}+{c}_{1},\\,{e}^{5\\cdot 0}+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨{c}_{1},\\,1+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=-2.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨4{t}^{4/3}+2,\\,{e}^{5t}-2⟩.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53fca396-4ade-5180-9c9b-a73f24f5b5b8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e6ee661-ba22-542d-a9fb-b76d8076a27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\ln \\mid t \\mid, \\frac{1}{t}⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095702} {:answer/id #uuid "bb21cdf1-22bd-524b-852c-992658713584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3\\ln \\mid t \\mid,-\\frac{5}{t}⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095703} {:answer/id #uuid "c82b3f55-eede-588c-8f00-ece0e818fa7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ⟨3\\ln \\mid t \\mid, \\frac{5}{t}⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095704} {:answer/id #uuid "499e839a-bf75-5fd9-bdc6-858357101649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\ln \\mid t \\mid,-\\frac{5}{t}⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095705} {:answer/id #uuid "57636129-14de-554a-a121-0e8f70645831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3\\ln \\mid t \\mid, \\frac{5}{t}⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb21cdf1-22bd-524b-852c-992658713584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3\\ln \\mid t \\mid,-\\frac{5}{t}⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095703} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c6edcd-4580-541c-8ae3-e21d3e20ddad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4a69144-d189-5ac0-bdfe-ac5dd5bddd37" :question/math-academy-id "q-50201" :question/problem "Find $\\int ⟨\\frac{3}{t},\\frac{5}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\frac{3}{t},\\,\\frac{5}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\frac{3}{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\frac{5}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{5}{t}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|,\\,-\\frac{5}{t}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|,\\,-\\frac{5}{t}⟩+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9688df89-056e-59d7-869e-9841715b2aea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b8e8a96-9973-5ffb-a692-45bf60b973a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332991} {:answer/id #uuid "14dd103f-69e8-5966-9e39-41a6ca90cf76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332992} {:answer/id #uuid "0edd8566-357e-5796-83ba-1da8b62e7650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332993} {:answer/id #uuid "267423a9-af09-5ebb-a456-596352097c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332994} {:answer/id #uuid "dca9ce97-ee20-5cbe-9fe4-8f2c57858563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14dd103f-69e8-5966-9e39-41a6ca90cf76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332992} :answer-field/id #uuid "de5a67a5-0e3d-5adc-88fa-817279c40f4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed9275a1-ce69-572b-90c1-a522c36d6a6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80463" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨{csc}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨{csc}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{csc}^{2}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int {csc}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53fca396-4ade-5180-9c9b-a73f24f5b5b8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c45f96d-03b1-5bd6-bb3a-31d5d9f60d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{({e}^{2t}+e)}{2}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333019} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b1a71d-f4b0-5165-94b6-4b0e922559d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333020} {:answer/id #uuid "166a60e6-716c-551b-a7dd-3beb0c13fae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{(2t+1)}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333021} {:answer/id #uuid "bcefa098-1f2f-539a-970b-dabe8993220c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333022} {:answer/id #uuid "728197e8-77cf-5e4a-a9a8-bb0d0d95bf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{2t}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcefa098-1f2f-539a-970b-dabe8993220c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333022} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b09ba27-ddf7-5f44-89a9-f9023f3c7f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb6f108b-b2c6-57c1-9132-69c2656eace2" :question/math-academy-id "q-99378" :question/problem "Find $\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3{e}^{(2t+1)}⟩\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{(2t+1)}⟩\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2{e}^{(2t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=2{e}^{(2t+1)},$ we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u=2t+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2{e}^{(2t+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 3{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{u}+{c}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3{e}^{(2t+1)}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3{e}^{(2t+1)}}{2}⟩+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
